# ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
# ║  DEVELOPMENT ONLY. THIS FILE SERVES CLEARTEXT HTTP ON localhost.           ║
# ║                                                                           ║
# ║  It is reachable only through `docker-compose.dev.yml`, which also sets    ║
# ║  NODE_ENV=development on the services behind it. Under NODE_ENV=production ║
# ║  those services refuse to start with an http:// APP_URL at all             ║
# ║  (packages/auth/src/transport.ts), so this file cannot be pointed at a     ║
# ║  production stack by editing one variable — which is the whole reason it   ║
# ║  is a second file rather than a flag in deploy/Caddyfile.                  ║
# ║                                                                           ║
# ║  It answers only loopback Host headers, and docker-compose.dev.yml         ║
# ║  publishes it on 127.0.0.1 only, so neither file alone can put it on a     ║
# ║  network. Do not copy this over deploy/Caddyfile.                          ║
# ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
#
# What it is for: reproducing the *proxy topology* on a laptop —
# `ATRIUM_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1`, one proxy appending to `X-Forwarded-For`, no
# published ports on `app` or `server`. That is the arrangement the rate
# limiter's per-address dimension depends on, and the only honest way to
# exercise it locally is to run it. TLS is the part that is missing here, and it
# is missing because a self-signed certificate on localhost teaches nobody
# anything about whether the production path works.
#
# The ordinary local loop does not need this file at all: `pnpm infra:up &&
# pnpm dev` runs both apps directly on :3000 and :4000.
{
	admin off
	# No automatic HTTPS: there is no hostname here to get a certificate for.
	auto_https off
}

# Loopback names only. Round 5 wrote `:80`, which answers to any Host header, so
# the only thing between this cleartext stack and the internet was the compose
# file's port publishing — one variable away from `0.0.0.0`. That is now
# `127.0.0.1:...` in docker-compose.dev.yml, and this is the second half.
#
# Measured, not assumed: `caddy adapt` turns these three addresses into
# `"listen": [":80"]` plus `"match": [{"host": ["localhost","127.0.0.1","::1"]}]`.
# The site address is a **Host matcher**, not a bind — and it has to be, because
# binding the container's own loopback would make docker's published port
# unreachable. The bind that matters is the host-side `127.0.0.1:` above.
# All three names are listed because a laptop reaches this over any of them and
# a 404 on `http://127.0.0.1:3000` would be a bad way to learn that.
localhost:80, 127.0.0.1:80, [::1]:80 {
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}

	handle /ws* {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	handle /attachments/* {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	handle /{$S3_BUCKET}/* {
		reverse_proxy minio:9000
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy app:3000
	}
}

# Anything arriving under some other name is refused, out loud.
#
# The site block above was written first, on its own, with a comment claiming a
# foreign Host "gets a 404 rather than a working plaintext login page". Measured
# against a running container, that was wrong: Caddy answers an unmatched Host
# with an empty `200 OK`. No app content, but a live port that says "fine" — and
# a proxy or a scanner in front of this would read that as a healthy service.
#
# 421 Misdirected Request is the accurate code: the request reached a server
# that will not answer for that authority. The block is keyless, so it is the
# fallback the specific host-matched block above always outranks — verified by
# probing all four Hosts against the running pair rather than by reading the
# routing rules.
:80 {
	respond "atrium dev proxy: this stack answers on localhost only (docker-compose.dev.yml)" 421
}
